    "case_id": "188",
    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old woman with a 7-year history of a submucosal tumour below the esophagogastric junction, monitored periodically since 2004, presented in November 2011 with melena and progressive anemia; her hemoglobin level was 7.5 g/dL. Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen revealed a 70 × 50 × 55 mm hypoechogenic, subcardial mass on the posterior gastric wall. In December 2011, the lesion was resected endoscopically by a piecemeal technique under general anesthesia, and histopathology reportedly confirmed a lipoma. Six weeks later, endoscopic evaluation demonstrated a recurrent bleeding tumour at the same site.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Liposarcoma\nB. Benign lipoma was initially diagnosed on resection, but the tumour recurred soon after\nC. Gastrointestinal stromal tumour was considered in the differential for a submucosal gastric mass, but the CT showed a\nD. Lymphoma was included in the differential, but the absence of lymphadenopathy on imaging and the fatty nature of the\nE. Peritoneal liposarcoma adjacent to the stomach was considered, but the lesion arose within the submucosal and muscular\nF. Hepatic metastasis abutting the stomach was considered, but CT did not demonstrate any secondary lesions in the liver\nG. None\nH. None\n",
